{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs is set to end, and a new law will make possessing small amounts of hard drugs a crime again. The new law gives those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Those found with personal-use amounts could face up to six months in jail. The law aims to crack down on public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling near parks.\n\n*** \nSupporters argue that the law allows counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources. However, opponents claim it may create an inconsistent policy landscape. The law encourages counties to create treatment alternatives but does not make it mandatory.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, those eligible for deflection will be connected to a mobile behavioral health team. If the team fails to arrive within 30 minutes, the individual will be taken to jail. As the country navigates this shift, it's worth considering the broader implications on individuals of all age groups. Some may need to relearn how to navigate societal norms, much like learning to eat with a fork or going shopping with a list, and finding the right words to speak one's tongue. \nP.P.S As the new law takes shape, it's clear that the road ahead will be complex, with many factors to consider, from the impact on the country's youth to how people will adapt to the changes, and perhaps even how they will choose to spend their time, whether that's going shopping or simply being mindful of their tongue when speaking.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -1.5139465176616795e-05}, {"token": "regon", "logprob":